  <details class="methodology">
    <summary>Methodology &amp; formulas</summary>
    <div class="methodology-body">
      <h3>Growth models</h3>
      <p><strong>Linear</strong> \u2014 ordinary least-squares trend line through all historical points, extended forward at a constant absolute increment per year.</p>
      <p><strong>Exponential</strong> \u2014 OLS regression on the log of historical values, equivalent to the constant % growth rate that best fits the whole history. Falls back to Linear if any historical value is zero or negative.</p>
      <p><strong>Avg YoY %</strong> \u2014 the mean of historical year-over-year growth rates, compounded forward from the last actual value.</p>
      <p><strong>Custom</strong> \u2014 you set a growth % for each projected year directly (e.g. a decelerating glide path).</p>
      <p>Any individual projected cell can also be typed over directly \u2014 that becomes a manual override (highlighted in brass) and sticks regardless of the model, until you hit <code>reset</code>.</p>
      <h3>DCF</h3>
      <p><code>PV of FCF</code> = FCF &divide; (1 + WACC)<sup>year</sup>. <code>Terminal value</code> = final-year FCF &times; (1 + terminal growth) &divide; (WACC &minus; terminal growth), discounted back the same way. <code>Enterprise value</code> = sum of PV(FCF) + PV(terminal value). <code>Equity value</code> = enterprise value &minus; net debt. <code>Intrinsic value / share</code> = equity value &divide; diluted shares.</p>
      <h3>Comps cross-check</h3>
      <p>P/E method: peer average P/E &times; (final projected-year net income &divide; shares). EV/EBITDA method: (peer average EV/EBITDA &times; final projected-year EBITDA &minus; net debt) &divide; shares.</p>
      <h3>Data</h3>
      <p>Fetched from Financial Modeling Prep's free tier (income statement, cash flow, balance sheet, profile, quote). Free-tier accounts are generally limited to US-listed tickers and roughly five years of annual history \u2014 everything is editable if you need more, less, or a correction.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <footer class="disclaimer">
    This is a modeling aid, not financial advice \u2014 all figures, growth models, and assumptions are yours to edit, and the output is only as good as the inputs. Verify against primary filings before relying on this for an investment decision.
  </footer>

</div>

